# HEAL FUNCTION INTRODUCTION #
##############################
# This is the preferred directory to put your heal scripts.
# The use case could be in a scenario described below.
# Consider you have an application that runs into a bad state and your plugin check returns warning and critical.
# If you for instance know that a restart of the application will restore it to a good state you can use a heal script to (try to) restart it.
# You will need to toggle the Almond setting 

almond.tryToHeal = true

# in the almond.conf file and you need to add a settings to heal.conf file, for instance like this:

{
  "react_on_state": "WARNING",
  "heals": [
    {
      "plugin": "check_application",
      "run": "/opt/almond/scripts/heal_application",
      "timeout_seconds": 30
    }
  ]
}

# if the above example the plugin "check_application" will trigger a run of the script "/opt/almond/scripts/heal_application" if the check state changes
# from OK to WARNING. If the script doesn´t return a result in 30 seconds Almond will see the heal script execution as a failure.
# Take note, Almond will only try to execute a heal script (the triggered script) once.
# Also take note that if tryToHeal is enabled Almond will try to find a heal script for every state change away from OK. If no script is available for
# the event Almond will return a "failed to heal" in the logs.
# Finally take note that Almond does not come with predefined heal scripts. This is all up to the user to enable and implement if you have good use for it.
